-- Leak guard for the per-ACCUMULATOR box leak in the compiled fold helpers
-- (march_typed_array_fold / native_float_arr_fold / native_f32_arr_fold in
-- runtime/march_runtime.c).
--
-- Sibling of native_arr_fold_leak_probe.march, which guards the per-ELEMENT
-- box. That probe deliberately uses an INT accumulator so it measures the
-- element box in isolation; this one is the other half and deliberately uses
-- a FLOAT accumulator, which is the shape that leaks here.
--
-- WHAT LEAKED. All the fold helpers carry the accumulator through the loop in
-- the erased/boxed representation:
--
--     result = call_closure_2(f, result, elem);
--
-- and never released the PREVIOUS result. When the closure allocates its
-- result -- and for a Float accumulator it always does, see below -- that is
-- one heap object dropped on the floor per element, unbounded in array
-- length. Measured at ~32 B/element: a 5,000,000-element Float fold cost
-- 193.6 MB against a 40.4 MB Int-accumulator control.
--
-- WHY RELEASING THE PREVIOUS ACCUMULATOR NEEDS A WITNESS, AND WHAT FLOAT'S IS.
-- An apply fn does NOT return an owned reference in general. For a `ptr`
-- parameter returned unchanged it emits a bare `ret ptr %x` with no inc_rc --
-- i.e. it hands back a BORROWED alias of a value someone else still owns
-- (an array element, a closure capture). Releasing such a "previous
-- accumulator" would free a live object.
--
-- A Float is different, and provably so: the uniform-ptr ABI unboxes a Float
-- parameter to a raw `double` in the apply fn's entry prologue
-- (Llvm_toplevel.emit_fn) and re-boxes on return via `march_alloc_float`
-- (Llvm_ctx.coerce). Float closure CAPTURES are likewise stored as raw
-- doubles. So every Float-typed value coming out of an apply fn is a FRESH
-- box that the fold loop solely owns -- there is no path by which it aliases
-- the array, the caller's accumulator, or a capture. That is why the release
-- in the runtime is guarded on MARCH_FLOAT_TAG rather than applied to every
-- accumulator: the tag is a witness of "this box was certainly freshly
-- allocated by the callee" that the runtime can read off the VALUE, with no
-- help from the compiler.
--
-- THE OTHER HALF: A HEAP NON-FLOAT ACCUMULATOR. There is no tag that says
-- "freshly allocated String", so for those the COMPILER supplies the witness
-- instead: Borrow inference already knows whether an apply fn has any OWNING
-- use of its accumulator parameter, and lib/tir/clo_flags.ml stamps that one
-- bit (MARCH_CLO_ARG0_BORROWED) into the closure object's header pad word.
-- Set = the callee neither consumed nor retained the accumulator we passed,
-- so the fold loop still holds the only reference and must release it. The
-- string_acc_* legs below pin that half; they leaked exactly one String per
-- element before it (numbers at the bottom of this header).
--
-- Both witnesses are still needed. The Float one is intrinsic to the value
-- and survives every path that builds a closure; the stamped one is absent
-- (and the release correctly does not happen) whenever a closure is built by
-- a path that does not stamp -- REPL/JIT fragments, hot reload, the
-- cross-heap message copier.
--
-- ACCUMULATOR TYPE IS LOAD-BEARING, in the opposite direction from the
-- sibling probe, and it is the whole reason this defect hid twice. A
-- wire-tagged Int accumulator is an IMMEDIATE: never heap-allocated, so it
-- cannot exhibit this leak at all -- which is why all four in-tree stdlib
-- callers of typed_array_fold (stdlib/dataframe.march, col_null_count) are
-- Int-accumulator and why nothing in-tree caught either half. Every leg here
-- must keep a HEAP accumulator: Float for the tag-witnessed half, String for
-- the compiler-witnessed half. Do NOT "simplify" any of them to an Int --
-- the probe would still pass, measuring nothing.
--
-- THE CLOSURE BODY IS LOAD-BEARING TOO, for the String legs specifically.
-- The stamped bit is only set when borrow inference can see that the
-- accumulator has no owning use, so the accumulator may only be passed to
-- callees whose borrow modes are DECLARED (borrow.ml's extern_borrow_table:
-- string_starts_with is, string_length is not) or ignored outright. Rewriting
-- `string_starts_with(acc, "s")` into `string_length(acc)` below turns the
-- accumulator owned, clears the bit, and re-leaks -- correctly, since a
-- consuming callee is exactly the case that must NOT be released.
--
-- SIGNAL: live_allocs(), NOT RSS -- see the long rationale in
-- native_arr_fold_leak_probe.march. Short version: an absolute RSS threshold
-- is not portable (a healthy Linux process baseline is ~122 MB and went RED
-- on CI), while live_allocs() counts the defect directly.
--
-- THE LAST THREE LEGS ARE DOUBLE-FREE WITNESSES, not leak witnesses, and they
-- are the reason the naive fix is not what landed. `if (result != acc)
-- march_decrc(prev)` is wrong in every way they pin:
--
--   * identity_leg -- the closure returns its accumulator ARGUMENT unchanged
--     (`fn (acc, _) -> acc`), so nothing fresh was ever allocated and
--     releasing the "previous" result would release a live object; and `base`
--     is still held by the caller and USED after the fold returns. The initial
--     accumulator belongs to the CALLER; a fold that releases it hands back a
--     dangling String and the trailing string_length(base) reads freed memory.
--   * elem_alias_leg -- the closure returns the ELEMENT, which the array owns
--     and the fold only borrows, so the chain of accumulators walks into the
--     array's own storage.
--   * consume_acc_leg -- the closure STORES the accumulator into its result,
--     which is the one shape where the callee really did take our reference
--     and the release must not happen at all.
--
-- A wire-tagged (non-pointer) accumulator must likewise never reach
-- march_decrc; the sibling probe's Int legs cover that direction.
--
-- Numbers measured on this fixture, Darwin arm64, --compile --opt 2:
--   fixed (both witnesses)            live_allocs delta =        20
--   whole compiler+runtime at 8897bb1a (i.e. before the closure borrow bit,
--   with the Float half already in)   live_allocs delta = 1,000,018
--   accumulator release removed altogether, measured 2026-08-20 on this
--   fixture's Float legs only         live_allocs delta = 4,000,007
-- Each leaked figure is exactly one object per element summed over the legs
-- that witness it (500,000 + 500,000 String; 2,000,000 + 2,000,000 Float),
-- i.e. the defect stated exactly rather than estimated. The dune rule asserts
-- < 1000, orders of magnitude above the healthy constant and below either
-- leaked figure, so it is falsifiable in both directions with an enormous
-- margin. NOTE that stdout is IDENTICAL in the leaking and fixed builds --
-- the .expected diff cannot catch this defect, only the delta can.
--
-- The healthy delta is a small CONSTANT, independent of element count -- not
-- an assumption: the Float half was measured at exactly 9 for 500,000,
-- 2,000,000 and 8,000,000 elements per leg, a 16x range. If the total ever
-- starts growing with the counts below, that is a real new leak, not drift in
-- this bound.
mod NativeArrFoldAccLeakProbe do
  needs IO.Console

  -- Float accumulator through native_float_arr_fold. Each iteration's
  -- `acc +. x` is a fresh march_alloc_float box that becomes the next
  -- accumulator.
  pfn f64_acc_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_float(2000000, 1.0)
    float_to_int(NativeArray.fold_float(arr, 0.0, fn (acc, x) -> acc +. x))
  end

  -- Same, through native_f32_arr_fold. The stored element is an f32 widened
  -- to double; the ACCUMULATOR is still a boxed Float.
  pfn f32_acc_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_f32(2000000, 1.0)
    float_to_int(NativeArray.fold_f32(arr, 0.0, fn (acc, x) -> acc +. x))
  end

  -- NOTE ON THE SHARED ROOT. march_typed_array_fold -- the reference
  -- implementation the three native_*_arr_fold helpers copied their RC
  -- discipline from -- cannot be given a FLOAT-accumulator leak leg here,
  -- because its compiled path is independently broken for exactly the
  -- accumulator types that would show it: `typed_array_fold(arr, 0,
  -- fn (acc, x) -> acc + x)` over five 7s returns 15 instead of 35, and the
  -- same fold with a Float accumulator SIGSEGVs. Both are pre-existing and
  -- unrelated to reference counting (the interpreter is correct on both),
  -- tracked in
  -- specs/todos/2026-08-20-typed-array-fold-compiled-scalar-accumulator-broken.md.
  -- Its HEAP-accumulator path is sound, and that is the one exercised here:
  -- string_acc_typed_leg (leak direction) plus identity_leg and
  -- elem_alias_leg (double-free direction).

  -- STRING accumulator through march_typed_array_fold -- the compiler-witnessed
  -- half. The closure READS the accumulator (through a callee whose borrow
  -- modes are declared) and returns a fresh, constant-length String, so every
  -- iteration drops one heap object on the floor unless the fold releases it.
  pfn string_acc_typed_leg() : Int do
    let arr = typed_array_create(500000, 7)
    let base = int_to_string(0)
    let r = typed_array_fold(arr, base,
      fn (acc, x) -> if string_starts_with(acc, "s") do int_to_string(x) else int_to_string(x + 1) end)
    string_length(base) + string_length(r)
  end

  -- Same, through native_int_arr_fold: the accumulator is a heap String while
  -- the ELEMENTS are wire-tagged immediates, so this leg isolates the
  -- accumulator chain from the per-element box the sibling probe measures.
  pfn string_acc_native_int_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_int(500000, 3)
    let base = int_to_string(0)
    let r = NativeArray.fold_int(arr, base, fn (_acc, x) -> int_to_string(x))
    string_length(base) + string_length(r)
  end

  -- DOUBLE-FREE WITNESS (see header). Not a leak leg: it exists to crash, or
  -- print a garbage length, if the accumulator release is over-eager.
  pfn identity_leg() : Int do
    let arr = typed_array_create(100000, 7)
    let base = int_to_string(10000000)
    let r = typed_array_fold(arr, base, fn (acc, _) -> acc)
    string_length(base) + string_length(r)
  end

  -- DOUBLE-FREE WITNESS #2: the closure returns the ELEMENT, which the ARRAY
  -- owns and this fold only borrows. The accumulator parameter is unused, so
  -- the borrow bit IS set and the release IS live here -- the reason this
  -- cannot free the array's element out from under it is the pointer-equality
  -- guard in march_typed_array_fold, which takes a reference of its own when
  -- the callee hands an element back. Reading an element AFTER the fold, and
  -- the length of the returned value, are what would fault or print garbage.
  pfn elem_alias_leg() : Int do
    let arr = typed_array_create(1000, int_to_string(4242))
    let base = int_to_string(9999)
    let r = typed_array_fold(arr, base, fn (_acc, x) -> x)
    string_length(r) + string_length(typed_array_get(arr, 0)) + string_length(base)
  end

  -- DOUBLE-FREE WITNESS #3: the closure CONSUMES the accumulator into its
  -- result (`Cons(x, acc)` stores the caller's reference with no inc_rc).
  -- The borrow bit must be CLEAR here; releasing the previous accumulator
  -- would free the tail of the list still being built.
  pfn consume_acc_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_int(200, 3)
    let r = NativeArray.fold_int(arr, Nil, fn (acc, x) -> Cons(x, acc))
    List.length(r)
  end

  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    let before = live_allocs()
    println(f64_acc_leg())
    println(f32_acc_leg())
    println(string_acc_typed_leg())
    println(string_acc_native_int_leg())
    println(identity_leg())
    println(elem_alias_leg())
    println(consume_acc_leg())
    let after = live_allocs()
    IO.warn(int_to_string(after - before))
  end
end
